1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,800
Hi — I'm

2
00:00:00,917 --> 00:00:04,083
Divij Pawar. This is part two of the Accountability Mesh series: the

3
00:00:04,083 --> 00:00:06,708
chain of trust. Last time, I proved structure holds.

4
00:00:07,208 --> 00:00:09,750
This time, I'm asking what else we can actually prove — and

5
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:11,000
where that still falls short.

6
00:00:11,780 --> 00:00:15,405
Last time I ended on this: 'Structure is enforceable. Truth, not yet.'

7
00:00:15,655 --> 00:00:18,280
Today's question: if we can't prove truth yet — what CAN we

8
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:20,030
actually prove? And is that enough?

9
00:00:20,870 --> 00:00:22,953
So I bolted on a new tool. Think of it as a

10
00:00:22,953 --> 00:00:24,370
security camera on the AI.

11
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:27,037
A camera doesn't know if you lied at the checkout — it

12
00:00:27,037 --> 00:00:29,453
just knows you were standing there, and what the receipt says.

13
00:00:29,953 --> 00:00:32,870
That's the deal here: this doesn't check if the AI's answer is

14
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:35,870
true. It records exactly what the AI actually did.

15
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:37,737
So now there's a chain of proof.

16
00:00:38,112 --> 00:00:39,695
Link one: did it actually call the tool?

17
00:00:40,362 --> 00:00:43,653
Camera-proven. Link two: was the data real, or made up? Provable. Link

18
00:00:43,653 --> 00:00:46,987
three: does a claimed number match the real financial filing? Checkable.

19
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:51,070
Link four: did the AI's stated reasoning actually cause its final answer?

20
00:00:51,362 --> 00:00:53,653
That link doesn't exist yet. Nobody's built it.

21
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:56,655
But a camera can be tricked. One — the AI can fetch

22
00:00:56,655 --> 00:00:59,988
real data right before answering, without the answer actually coming from that

23
00:00:59,988 --> 00:01:00,788
data.

24
00:01:00,905 --> 00:01:03,905
A rooster crows before the sun rises. The rooster doesn't cause the

25
00:01:04,155 --> 00:01:07,780
sunrise. Two — a longer, busier recording looks more impressive. Doing more

26
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:10,530
steps doesn't mean any step was done right. Three — if the

27
00:01:10,530 --> 00:01:13,697
wrong person gets to watch this footage, we've broken the exact rule

28
00:01:13,697 --> 00:01:15,322
we built the whole system to protect.

29
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,728
So here's the honest ceiling. We can now prove what the system

30
00:01:18,728 --> 00:01:21,770
did. We still can't prove it was right. That's not a failure.

31
00:01:22,062 --> 00:01:23,728
That's just where the line actually is.

32
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:27,388
So what would actually close that gap? Two ideas people are working

33
00:01:27,388 --> 00:01:28,188
on right now.

34
00:01:28,472 --> 00:01:30,430
One — pull a fact out of the evidence, like pulling a

35
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:32,972
block from a Jenga tower, and see if the answer falls over.

36
00:01:33,222 --> 00:01:35,638
If it doesn't move, that fact was never really holding it up.

37
00:01:35,972 --> 00:01:39,013
Two — look inside the AI's actual wiring instead of asking it

38
00:01:39,013 --> 00:01:39,847
to describe itself.

39
00:01:40,388 --> 00:01:43,055
Right now that only works on small models we're allowed to crack

40
00:01:43,055 --> 00:01:45,513
open — not the powerful ones we actually use.

41
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,122
There's a third idea, and it skips the reasoning question completely.

42
00:01:49,538 --> 00:01:52,538
Stop checking the reasoning at all — just track results over time,

43
00:01:52,747 --> 00:01:53,830
like a weather forecaster.

44
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:57,038
Nobody checks if a forecaster can explain the atmosphere.

45
00:01:57,413 --> 00:02:00,663
We check if it rains 80% of the time they say 80%.

46
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:04,210
So why hasn't this happened yet? The powerful models are locked boxes

47
00:02:04,293 --> 00:02:06,335
— we can't look inside the ones we actually use.

48
00:02:06,918 --> 00:02:10,293
Grading a track record takes real time. Months of real predictions. No

49
00:02:10,293 --> 00:02:13,543
shortcuts. And honestly, the biggest reason might not be technical at all.

50
00:02:14,002 --> 00:02:16,502
Right now it's cheaper and faster to build something that just LOOKS

51
00:02:16,502 --> 00:02:19,752
accountable — a shiny dashboard, a green checkmark — than to do

52
00:02:19,752 --> 00:02:21,460
the slow work of actually proving it.

53
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:24,677
This is also where I'm leaving this project before moving to something

54
00:02:24,677 --> 00:02:26,552
new — so here's the honest scorecard.

55
00:02:27,052 --> 00:02:28,510
What's real: the structure is enforced,

56
00:02:28,885 --> 00:02:32,010
claims are checked against real filings where possible, and now, what actually

57
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:33,177
happened is fully visible.

58
00:02:33,593 --> 00:02:36,968
What's still open: whether the reasoning is genuine. That's not solved. I'm

59
00:02:36,968 --> 00:02:39,677
not pretending it is. If I handed this to someone else tomorrow,

60
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:42,010
that's the one sentence I'd want them to read first.

61
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:47,375
Structure is enforceable. Behavior is observable. Truth — still open. Signing off,

62
00:02:47,375 --> 00:02:48,175
Divij Pawar.
